From jmacdon at med.umich.edu  Thu Apr 23 04:34:14 2009
From: jmacdon at med.umich.edu (James MacDonald)
Date: Wed, 22 Apr 2009 22:34:14 -0400
Subject: [BioC] Appropriate Probe annotation for HT-HGU133A chips
Message-ID: <49EF9B66020000EE00074201@gwia01x.med.umich.edu>

Hi Hannah,

The reason has to do with how Affy named these chips. Back in the day they had a chip called the HG-U133A (and B) that together were supposed to interrogate most of the known human gene transcripts. They then were able to reduce the feature size so that they could get all the content from the A and B chips on a single chip, plus about 5000 more genes. They called this the HG-U133_Plus2 chip. They also had an A chip called the HG-U133A_version2 that was smaller and cheaper - it had pretty much the same content (+/-) as the HG-U133A but used the smaller feature size.

When Affy came out with the HT chips, the first human one had these HG-U133_version2 chips on them. Given the maddeningly inconsistent way Affy names things, they called these the HT-HG-U133A instead of A_version2.

So since the chips are version 2, you should use the version 2 annotation.

Best,

Jim



